  "text": "Question three. Design a URL shortener at a high level. Start from the API: one call takes a long URL and returns a short key, and the other takes the key and redirects. The key is base sixty-two: lowercase, uppercase and digits. Encode an auto-incrementing ID, and six characters hold fifty-seven billion links, over fifteen years at ten million a day. Or hash the long URL, and check each key for a collision before you store it.\n\nStore the mapping in a key-value store, since every lookup is by key. Put a cache in front of it, because reads outnumber writes a hundred to one. And keep the app servers stateless, behind a load balancer, so scaling out is just adding one. Follow one redirect through it. On a cache hit, that's two round trips inside the data center, about a millisecond by the ladder. A miss adds a third, to the store, and an SSD read: about one point six milliseconds. The follow-up is the counter. One counter that every server asks is a bottleneck, and one more thing that can fail. Hand each server a block of IDs instead, say a million at a time, and it counts on its own. And counted IDs make consecutive keys, so anyone can walk through everyone's links. If that matters, scramble the ID before encoding it, or use the hash.",
